ments.\n    It uses a dynamic programming approach to build up a Bell triangle.\n    \"\"\"\n    if n == 0:\n        return 1\n\n    # Initialize a 2D array for the Bell triangle\n    # bell_triangle[i][j] will store S(i, j), Stirling numbers of the second kind\n    # and bell_triangle[i][0] will store B_i, the ith Bell number\n    bell_triangle = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n    # B_0 = 1\n    bell_triangle[0][0] = 1\n\n    for i in range(1, n + 1):\n        # S(i, 1) = 1\n        bell_triangle[i][1] = 1\n        # B_i = sum(S(i, k) for k=1 to i)\n        # B_i = S(i, 1) + sum(S(i, k) for k=2 to i)\n        current_bell_number = 1\n        for j in range(2, i + 1):\n            # S(i, j) = S(i-1, j-1) + j * S(i-1, j)\n            bell_triangle[i][j] = bell_triangle[i-1][j-1] + j * bell_triangle[i-1][j]\n            current_bell_number += bell_triangle[i][j]\n        \n        # B_i is the sum of S(i, k) for k from 1 to i\n        # However, it's also true that B_i = sum(S(i-1, k)) from k=0 to i-1\n        # or B_i = S(i,0) + S(i,1) + ... + S(i,i)\n        # A more common recurrence for Bell numbers directly from Stirling numbers:\n        # B_n = sum_{k=0}^{n-1} C(n-1, k) B_k\n        # Or using the Bell triangle recurrence: B_n = sum_{k=0}^{n-1} S(n,k)\n        # Where S(n,k) is Stirling numbers of second kind.\n        # The design context provides a simple recurrence B_n+1 = sum_{k=0 to n} C(n,k) B_k\n        # Let's use the property that B_n = S(n,0) + ... + S(n,n).\n        # Also, B_i can be derived from the first element of the next row.\n        # Bell numbers can be computed by the Aitken's array (Bell triangle):\n        # A_n,1 = B_n\n        # A_0,1 = 1\n        # A_i,j = A_i-1,j-1 + A_i,j-1\n        # Let's use the more standard Bell triangle which computes S(n, k) values.\n        # S(n, k) = S(n-1, k-1) + k * S(n-1, k)\n        # B_n = sum_{k=0 to n} S(n, k)\n\n        # The first element of row i of the Bell triangle (A_i,1 or B_i) is the sum of the previous row.\n        # Or, the left-most entry of row i is the right-most entry of row i-1.\n        # B_i = sum(S(i, k) for k=1 to i) as computed by current_bell_number,\n        # but also B_i = S(i,0) which is 1 if i=0 else 0\n        # No, B_i = sum_k S(i,k)\n        # A common way to form the Bell triangle where B_n is the first element of row n+1:\n        # T[0][0] = 1\n        # T[i][0] = T[i-1][i-1] for i > 0\n        # T[i][j] = T[i-1][j-1] + T[i][j-1] for j > 0\n        \n        # Let's restart with the standard Bell triangle definition where Bell numbers are B_n = T[n][0]\n        # T[i][0] = B_i\n        # T[i][j] = S(i, j+1)\n\n        # Let's use the explicit Stirling numbers computation\n        # S(n, k) counts partitions of a set of n elements into k non-empty subsets.\n        # B_n = sum(S(n, k) for k from 0 to n)\n        # S(n, 0) = 0 for n > 0, S(0, 0) = 1\n        # S(n, n) = 1\n        # S(n, k) = 0 for k > n or k < 0\n        # S(n, k) = S(n-1, k-1) + k * S(n-1, k)\n\n        # Create a table for Stirling numbers of the second kind, S[i][k]\n        S = [[0] * (n + 1) for _ in range(n + 1)]\n\n        # S(0, 0) = 1\n        S[0][0] = 1\n\n        for i in range(1, n + 1):\n            for k in range(1, i + 1):\n                # S(i, k) = S(i-1, k-1) + k * S(i-1, k)\n                S[i][k] = S[i-1][k-1] + k * S[i-1][k]\n        \n        # B_i = sum(S(i, k) for k from 0 to i)\n        # The Bell number B_n is the sum of the nth row of Stirling numbers.\n        current_bell_number = 0\n        for k in range(n + 1):\n            current_bell_number += S[n][k]\n        return current_bell_number", "base_status": "pass", "plus_status": "pass", "base_fail_tests": [], "plus_fail_tests": []}], "Mbpp/69": [{"task_id": "Mbpp/69", "solution": "def is_sublist(main: list, sub: list) -> bool:\n    \"\"\"Checks if 'sub' is a contiguous sublist within 'main'.\"\"\"\n    if not sub:\n        return False\n    \n    n = len(main)\n    m =